{"graph_chars": "The United States minimum wage is usually set close to the low-skill equilibrium, sometimes even a little below it. About one point five percent of hourly workers are paid the minimum. For teenagers and people without a diploma, it matters. In many cities, checkout clerks already earn more than the federal floor with no law forcing it. Typical estimate: a ten percent rise in the minimum wage cuts hiring of unskilled workers by one to two percent. Some studies find no effect at certain times and places — those are controversial. Walter Williams and Thomas Sowell argue a higher floor raises barriers for lower-skilled workers who would have learned on the job. If the floor sits just below equilibrium, it is nonbinding: the market still sets the wage. Even a small step above equilibrium creates only a small surplus. Double it toward living-wage levels, and the surplus of labor gets large. Clear It Up asks: if ninety-eight percent get a ten percent raise and two percent lose the job, is that good policy? It depends who loses the job — a parent, or a student on summer break — and it depends on hours, not only heads. Complex social problems rarely have simple answers.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0611], [0.0611, 0.1222], [0.1222, 0.1833], [0.1833, 0.2444], [0.2444, 0.3056], [0.3056, 0.3667], [0.3667, 0.4278], [0.4278, 0.4889], [0.4889, 0.55], [0.55, 0.6111], [0.6111, 0.6722], [0.6722, 0.7333], [0.7333, 0.7944], [0.7944, 0.8556], [0.8556, 0.9167], [0.9167, 0.9778], [0.9778, 1.0389], [1.0389, 1.1], [1.1, 1.1611], [1.1611, 1.2222], [1.2222, 1.2833], [1.2833, 1.3444], [1.3444, 1.4056], [1.4056, 1.4667], [1.4667, 1.5278], [1.5278, 1.5889], [1.5889, 1.65], [1.65, 1.7111], [1.7111, 1.7722], [1.7722, 1.8333], [1.8333, 1.8944], [1.8944, 1.9556], [1.9556, 2.0167], [2.0167, 2.0778], [2.0778, 2.1389], [2.1389, 2.2], [2.2, 2.2611], [2.2611, 2.3222], [2.3222, 2.3833], [2.3833, 2.4444], [2.4444, 2.5056], [2.5056, 2.5667], [2.5667, 2.6278], [2.6278, 2.6889], [2.6889, 2.75], 